; C runtime functions for memory management
; These are external functions from libc

; Aligned allocation is a PAIR through the shim, never libc's aligned_alloc +
; free (stage 7, brocken 5). Windows has no aligned_alloc at all — only
; _aligned_malloc, whose memory must be released with _aligned_free, and
; passing it to free() is undefined. One committed seed serves every target, so
; a Scaly side that named the libc pair would be right on three targets and
; corrupt the heap on the fourth. The two halves therefore travel together;
; see the block at the end of scaly/fiber/eio.c.
; Note: Using i64 for size parameters since Scaly integer literals are signed
function scaly_aligned_alloc(alignment: i64, size: i64) returns pointer[void] extern

; Fault injection for the out-of-memory gates (scaly/fiber/eio.c): the
; (n+1)-th following aligned allocation answers null, once; -1 disarms. It is
; a declaration in the PRELUDE because the gates are ordinary programs, and it
; exists for the reason SCALYC_STACK_BUDGET exists in the planner -- a trap no
; test can fire is a trap that may have stopped working.
procedure scaly_alloc_fail_after(n: i64) extern
function scaly_aligned_free(ptr: pointer[void]) extern
function exit(code: i32) extern
procedure abort() extern

; I/O and string functions
function strlen(s: pointer[const_char]) returns size_t extern
function write(fd: int, buf: pointer[void], count: size_t) returns size_t extern
; POSIX read(2). Returns bytes read, 0 at EOF, or (size_t)-1 on error.
; Unreferenced by the scaly package itself, so it emits no `declare` into
; scaly.ll (fixpoint-safe); consumers (e.g. the LSP stdin transport)
; reference it and get the declaration in their own module.
function read(fd: int, buf: pointer[void], count: size_t) returns size_t extern
function memset(dest: pointer[void], value: i32, count: size_t) returns pointer[void] extern
function memcpy(dest: pointer[void], src: pointer[void], count: size_t) returns pointer[void] extern

; ---- Opaque C stream/directory handles -------------------------------------
; C names these types; it does not hand out `void*` for them. `FILE` is
; `typedef struct _IO_FILE FILE;` and `DIR` is a struct too, so `pointer[void]`
; here was our laziness, not the prototype's. The empty concept carries the
; IDENTITY (the C-header idiom this tree already uses for the LLVM handles),
; which makes `fclose(dirp)` a planner error instead of a silent run.
; ABI-identical: an empty concept behind a pointer lowers to a bare `ptr`.
; Declared in the PRELUDE so there is ONE declaration per opaque kind for the
; whole tree — every package sees these without a `use`.
; --- the value a `try` catches -------------------------------------------
;
; A runtime fault is what a trap USED to be: `exit 15` ended the process, and
; nothing could look at it (TRAPS.md 0). Since 2026-09-07 a `try` can catch
; one, and these are the types its arms select on:
;
;     try let x a[i]
;         when e: OutOfBounds
;             report(e.index, e.length)
;
; ★It is a UNION and not a record because that is what `when` arms are: the
; planner resolves an arm name to a variant tag of the scrutinee's union
; (plan_when -> lookup_variant_tag), so a single record would have no arm to
; select. Five variants today; the rest of the trap family in TRAPS.md 2.4 --
; an oversized page, the fiber codes -- each needs its own decision about
; whether it MAY be caught at all, which is why they are not here yet.
;
; ★`InvalidRegion` is the one kind that is not a programmer error in the
; caller's own code: a RECEIVED region is data from somewhere else, and a
; long-running process must be able to lose the message instead of itself.
; ★★It says nothing about CLEANUP -- what a catcher owes there depends on
; where the fault was raised, and `Region.deserialize`'s comment is the place
; that spells it out.
;
; ★The names are deliberately specific: everything here lives in the prelude,
; where a short name like `Bounds` would be taken away from every program in
; the tree (CLAUDE.md, prelude names are TAKEN).
;
; ★★★THE ORDER IS A CONTRACT WITH scaly/memory/panic.c: a variant's TAG is its
; position here, and the shim answers that tag from `scaly_panic_last_kind`
; and fills the matching payload in `scaly_panic_fill`. Appending a kind means
; appending it in BOTH files in the same order -- out of step, a `when` arm
; binds another kind's payload and nothing says a word. The shim carries the
; same note with the constants written out.
;
; ★`where` is the enclosing symbol's MANGLED name and not a source location:
; a run has no source position, and a mangled name is what a reader can grep
; for. It is a `pointer[const_char]` because that is what the C shim hands
; over -- a static string in the module that raised, so it outlives every
; region the jump unwinds.
define OutOfBoundsFault (index: size_t, length: size_t)
define NullReferenceFault (where: pointer[const_char])
define InvalidRegionFault (what: pointer[const_char], address: size_t)
define SizeOverflowFault (added: size_t, length: size_t)
define OutOfMemoryFault (what: pointer[const_char], bytes: size_t)

define RuntimeFault union
(
    OutOfBounds: OutOfBoundsFault,
    NullReference: NullReferenceFault,
    InvalidRegion: InvalidRegionFault,
    SizeOverflow: SizeOverflowFault,
    OutOfMemory: OutOfMemoryFault
)

define OpaqueFile()
define OpaqueDir()

; File I/O functions (stdio.h)
function fopen(filename: pointer[const_char], mode: pointer[const_char]) returns pointer[OpaqueFile] extern
function fclose(stream: pointer[OpaqueFile]) returns i32 extern
function fread(ptr: pointer[void], size: size_t, count: size_t, stream: pointer[OpaqueFile]) returns size_t extern
function fwrite(ptr: pointer[void], size: size_t, count: size_t, stream: pointer[OpaqueFile]) returns size_t extern
; Seeking and telling go through the eio shim, NOT through libc's fseek/ftell.
; Those carry `long` offsets — 64-bit on LP64, 32-bit on LLP64 (Win64) — and one
; committed seed serves every target, so a single declaration cannot describe
; both. `ftell` is how File.scaly measures a file's SIZE, so a 32-bit result
; would cap every source file at 2 GB and turn the `< 0` error test into a coin
; flip. The 64-bit variants exist everywhere but under OS-specific names
; (ftello/fseeko vs _ftelli64/_fseeki64), which is containment rule (a).
; See packages/scaly/0.1.0/scaly/fiber/eio.c and check 4 of tests/abi/run.sh.
function scaly_eio_seek(stream: pointer[OpaqueFile], offset: int, whence: int) returns int extern
function scaly_eio_tell(stream: pointer[OpaqueFile]) returns int extern
; rewind() takes no offset and returns nothing, so it has no `long` to get
; wrong and stays a direct extern.
function rewind(stream: pointer[OpaqueFile]) extern

; Directory functions (dirent.h)
function opendir(name: pointer[const_char]) returns pointer[OpaqueDir] extern
function closedir(dirp: pointer[OpaqueDir]) returns i32 extern

; File system functions (unistd.h)
function access(path: pointer[const_char], mode: int) returns i32 extern
function unlink(path: pointer[const_char]) returns i32 extern
function rmdir(path: pointer[const_char]) returns i32 extern
function mkdir(path: pointer[const_char], mode: int) returns i32 extern

; Subprocess spawn (stdlib.h) - runs command via /bin/sh
function system(command: pointer[const_char]) returns i32 extern

; Path functions (libgen.h)
function dirname(path: pointer[char]) returns pointer[char] extern
function basename(path: pointer[char]) returns pointer[char] extern

; String comparison (string.h)
function strcmp(s1: pointer[const_char], s2: pointer[const_char]) returns i32 extern
function memcmp(s1: pointer[void], s2: pointer[void], n: size_t) returns i32 extern

; Page allocator entry points are defined in scaly/memory/root_pages.scaly
; (Scaly). The only C surface left is in scalyc/page_runtime.cpp: state
; holder + atexit + LIFO-abort helper, which Scaly imports as scaly_rt_*.
